# A structure template is binary (gzip-framed NBT): never diffed or normalised as
# text. The engine tracks only the test pieces its tests bind; the prefab
# library is the content repository's, through git-lfs there.
*.nbt binary

# For Windows compatibility.
# Shell and Dockerfiles are executed inside Linux containers, but a Windows checkout would turn them into CRLF.
# Force LF in the working tree on every platform.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
Dockerfile.* text eol=lf
**/Dockerfile.* text eol=lf

# The kernel reads the same first line for every interpreter, not only for bash.
# The class is every tracked file some machine runs through line one — one that
# carries a shebang, or one that carries the executable bit. That population is
# derived from the tree by `tools/ci/check-eol-attributes.py`, which refuses a file
# no pattern here covers, so a new extension reds on the pull request rather
# than on the Windows checkout that cannot run it.
# `*.py` covers every python in the tree; it leaves out a python program named
# without that extension, of which this repository tracks none.
# `*.mjs` covers the measurement spikes; it leaves out `.js`, `.cjs` and `.ts`,
# none of which carry a shebang or the executable bit here.
*.py text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
